                <h2 class="section-title">CI/CD Integration</h2>
                <!-- <p class="section-subtitle">ConScan leverages well-established open-source security and compliance tools</p> -->

                <h3 id="github-action">Integration With Github Action</h3>
                <p class="para-content">
                    We can easily use this conscan tool with github action. With the github action the html reports are generated and these reports can be published directly to GitHub Pages using GitHub Actions, no manual steps required 🚀.
                </p>
                
                <p>This makes it super easy to:</p>
                <div style="padding-left: 30px;">
                    <p>✅ Automate container image vulnerability scans</p>
                    <p>✅ Host & share scan results securely</p>
                    <p>✅ Integrate with your CI/CD workflow seamlessly</p>
                </div>

                <br>
                <p>Here’s a quick example of how it works:</p>
                <ul style="padding-left: 30px;">
                    <li>Actions runs on each push</li> 
                    <li>conscan scans all container images</li>
                    <li>Reports are committed and published automatically to the eg. gh-pages branch</li>
                    <li>Accessible instantly at GitHub Pages link.</li>
                </ul>
                
                <br>    
                <p class="para-content">
                    I have setup <a href="https://github.com/peek8/conscan-sample">a github repo named conscan-sample</a> where we are building some sample images (those images doesn't have any real use case, just for testing) 
                    and scanning them with <a href="https://github.com/peek8/conscan">conscan tool</a> and publish the report to github pages.
                    For more details, See the scan Job and update-index Job at the <a href="https://github.com/peek8/conscan-sample/blob/main/.github/workflows/build.yaml#L63-L111">github action workflows at conscan-sample repo</a>.

                    The generated reports are available at <a href="https://peek8.github.io/conscan-sample/conscan-reports/">Conscan-sample Github Pages</a>.
               </p>
               <p class="para-content">
                    Isn't it awesome, You can actually do a lot without doing almost anything (other than copy/paste) :).
                    <br>
                    Cheers, Happy Scanning !! 🎉 🎉
               </p>

            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- CLI Reference -->
        <section class="section-light" id="cli-ref">
            <div class="container">
                <h2 class="section-title">CLI Referece</h2>
                <h3>Synopsis</h3>
                <p class="para-content">
                    Scanner for a container image for vulnerabilities, exposed secrets, inefficient file storage, installed packages and CIS(Center for Internet Security) Benchmarks.
                </p>
                <div class="code-block">
                    <pre>
conscan scan [flags]  target 
                    </pre>
                </div>
                <h3>Examples</h3>
                <div class="code-block">
                    <pre>
# Scan a container image locally available
$ conscan scan yourimage:tag          // uses the Podman/Docker daemon for local images

# Scan container images from registry eg. dockerhub
$ conscan scan docker.io/yourimage:tag

# or from github image repo
$ conscan scan ghcr.io/yourimage:tag

# By default the scan report will be in Table format that is more convenient for the Console output. 
# If you want the report in json format, you can use
$ conscan scan --format json -o report.json yourimage:tag # the report will be saved to report.json

# Similary to save in html format:
$ conscan scan --format html -o report.html yourimage:tag

# By default, conscan will scan everything. If you are interested for specific scan report, eg only vulnerabilities and exposed secrets you can use like:
$ conscan scan --scanners=vuln,secret yourimage:tag
                    </pre>
                </div>
                <h3>Options</h3>
                <div class="code-block">
                    <pre>
  -f, --format string      Format of the scanning report (default "table")
  -h, --help               help for scan
  -o, --output string      Output File name (optional)
      --scanners strings   Comma-separated list of what type of scans to be performed (allowed values: [vuln secret package cis storage all]).
                           By default, Every scan is performed. Except for 'table format', "Scanning" Packages is  excluded for the sake of brevity while the report is not written to Console (default [all])   
                    </pre>
                </div>  
            </div>
        </section>

